class:: RenewalTrig
summary:: Renewal-process events with selectable interval distributions
categories:: UGens>Triggers>Stochastic
related:: Guides/Stochastica, Classes/HawkesTrig, Classes/Dust

description::

A renewal process is a fundamental stochastic model for events occurring over time. Mathematically, it generates triggers where the wait time between each successive trigger is drawn independently from a specific probability distribution. Conceptually, this is the core engine of stochastic rhythm. By shaping the distribution of the waiting times, you can create highly organic, non-grid beats, simulating everything from the steady, memoryless ticking of a Geiger counter to the clustered dropping of raindrops.

Schedules independent inter-event intervals from exponential, gamma, Weibull, lognormal, or Erlang distributions. The random component has mean code::1/rate:: and code::minInterval:: is added as a refractory duration.

The first interval is scheduled without an event. At most one event is emitted per calculation and negative timing remainder is carried. Like link::Classes/Dust::, RenewalTrig generates stochastic impulses; unlike Dust, it exposes and controls the complete interval law.

subsection:: Event and interval view

image::renewal-process-events.png#Left: event times from an exponential renewal process at rate = 4 with minInterval = 0.05. Right: the empirical interval density; the hard empty region before 0.05 is the refractory period.::

classmethods::

method:: ar
argument:: rate
Reciprocal mean of the random interval in events per second. Nonpositive disables events.
argument:: distribution
Code: 0 exponential, 1 gamma, 2 Weibull, 3 lognormal, 4 Erlang.
argument:: shape
Positive distribution shape, or log-domain deviation for lognormal.
argument:: minInterval
Nonnegative refractory seconds added after sampling.
argument:: seed
Init-rate local seed.
argument:: reset
Restore the seed and schedule a new first interval without emitting.
returns:: Trigger and currently scheduled interval seconds.

method:: kr
Control-rate scheduler with at most one event per control block.

instancemethods::

examples::

code::
(
{ Decay2.ar(RenewalTrig.ar(8)[0], 0.001, 0.04) * 0.1 }.play
)
::

code::
(
{ Decay2.ar(RenewalTrig.ar(12, 1, 4)[0], 0.001, 0.03) * WhiteNoise.ar(0.1) }.play
)
::

code::
(
{
    var event = RenewalTrig.ar(10, 2, 0.7, 0.03)[0];
    SinOsc.ar(TExpRand.ar(200, 3000, event), 0, Decay2.ar(event, 0.001, 0.08) * 0.1)
}.play
)
::

code::
// Stochastic Raindrops
(
{
    var trig, freq, sig;
    // Uses a Gamma distribution for inter-onset times, avoiding rigid grid structures
    trig = RenewalTrig.kr(rate: 10, shape: 0.5);
    freq = TRand.kr(400, 2000, trig);
    sig = Klank.ar(`[[freq, freq*1.5, freq*2.3], nil, [1, 1, 1]], Decay.kr(trig, 0.05) * PinkNoise.ar(0.1));
    Pan2.ar(sig * 0.1)
}.play;
)
::
